      "side": 1,
      "message": "Oh, I\u0027m so glad you asked 😊\n\nI\u0027m debugging some discrepancies in our fractal noise impl vs. AE, and kind of narrowed it down to octaves composition/averaging.\n\nMore specifically, the \"sub influence\" property determines how much each noise layer contributes to the result and, per Adobe [1], \"At 100%, all iterations have the same amount of influence. At 50%, each iteration has half as much influence as the previous iteration. A value of 0% makes the effect appear exactly as if Complexity is 1.\"\n\nThat sounds like weighted average (which is what we have now), and for 100% specifically it sounds like plain arithmetic mean.\n\nBut as you increase the number of planes, averaging converges to midtone, and the noise softens quite a bit.  That\u0027s not what AE does though, theirs remains relatively sharp and retains details.\n\nAveraging also doesn\u0027t make sense fundamentally: we\u0027re composing noise signals, so if e.g. two octaves yield positive values then the result should increase -- but averaging will reduce it instead.\n\nSo yeah, I\u0027m struggling to figure how AE composes noise planes and visualizing the color distribution (how it shrinks with more planes) while I tweak the impl is super helpful.\n\n\n[1] https://helpx.adobe.com/after-effects/using/noise-grain-effects.html",
